// -*- C++ -*-  THE DEFAULT KEY BINDINGS. Seeded from `Features.md` §8, row for row.
//
// §8 is the SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH for key bindings; this file is its executable form and must not
// drift from it. `tst_keybindings` parses §8 out of the Markdown and asserts that every row here has
// a row there and vice versa (decision D1-9), so a documentation edit the code has not absorbed
// breaks the build. That strictness is the point: the alternative -- a hand-copied list nobody
// checks -- fails *silently*, which is strictly worse.
//
//   RAWPAGE_BINDING(Context, "Chord", ActionEnumerator)
//   RAWPAGE_BINDING_NATIVE(Context, "Chord", "why the focused widget owns this")
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY THERE ARE TWO MACROS -- and the rule that decides which one a row gets.
//
// Some §8 rows are not commands. `↑ / ↓ / ← / →` in the Page Thumbnails pane means "move the
// selection", which is what a `QListView` already does; `Tab` in Forms — Fill means "next field",
// which is the focus chain. Giving those an `ActionId` would mint permanent public names for
// behaviours nothing will ever dispatch -- and every `ActionId` string is permanent, because a
// user's saved shortcuts key off it (D1-16).
//
// THE TEST: a row is widget-native if it moves focus, selection, or scroll INSIDE the focused
// widget and has no meaning outside an interactive session. Everything else is dispatched. That
// line is drawn from `CLAUDE.md`'s standing rule -- *no feature the headless batch path cannot
// reach* -- read in the other direction: arrow-key list navigation is an interaction, not a
// feature, and a batch run would never invoke it. "Delete the selected pages" is a feature.
//
// A native row is still RECORDED, so §8 stays accounted for row by row and the test can hold both
// halves to the same standard. Silently dropping these rows would make "the store matches §8" true
// only of the rows I found convenient.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

#ifndef RAWPAGE_BINDING
#define RAWPAGE_BINDING(Context, Chord, Enumerator)
#endif

#ifndef RAWPAGE_BINDING_NATIVE
#define RAWPAGE_BINDING_NATIVE(Context, Chord, Reason)
#endif

